    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Advanced optimal control\"\ncourse-unit: ch14-advanced-optimal-control\nstatus: complete\n---\n\n## From a timing decision to a control function\n\n- The interventions and optimization chapter chooses one intervention start time.\n- Here $u(t)$ is a bounded, function-valued intervention.\n- Classification: deterministic, continuous-time, continuous-state,\n  population-level, ODE-constrained.\n\nUse normalized states $S,I,C$ with $S(0)=0.99$, $I(0)=0.01$ and $C(0)=0$.\nThe horizon is $T=20$, $0\\leq u\\leq0.5$, and $\\int_0^T u(t)\\,dt\\leq2$.\n\n::: {.notes}\nThe support points used by the transcription are numerical degrees of freedom;\nthey do not make the mathematical control discrete.\n:::\n\n## Controlled SIR and objective\n\n$$\n\\dot S=-(1-u)\\beta cSI,\\quad\n\\dot I=(1-u)\\beta cSI-\\gamma I,\\quad\n\\dot C=(1-u)\\beta cSI.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\min_u C(T)+10^{-3}\\int_0^T u(t)^2dt\n\\quad\\text{s.t.}\\quad\n0\\le u(t)\\le u_{\\max},\\quad \\int_0^T u(t)dt\\le B.\n$$\n\nThe identity $C(t)=S(0)-S(t)$ is a structural validation.\n\n## Current InfiniteOpt formulation\n\n::: {#2 .cell execution_count=1}\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\n\n******************************************************************************\nThis program contains Ipopt, a library for large-scale nonlinear optimization.\n Ipopt is released as open source code under the Eclipse Public License (EPL).\n         For more information visit https://github.com/coin-or/Ipopt\n******************************************************************************\n\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.notes}\nThis is the supported InfiniteOpt 0.6 path in the root environment. Ipopt\nreturns a local solution to the collocated nonlinear program.\n:::\n\n## Read the transcription and plot it\n\n::: {#4 .cell execution_count=1}\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<img width=850 height=350 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n```\n\nCollocated SIR trajectory and the optimized bounded intervention over time.\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\nCheck bounds, nonnegativity, and the incidence identity before interpretation.\nRefining supports is the relevant numerical convergence experiment.\n\n## Capacity as a path constraint\n\n$$\nI(t) \\le I_{\\max}\\quad\\text{for all } t\n$$\n\nConstrains the whole trajectory, not just the endpoint. Three regimes:\n\n| Cap | Status | Final cases | Peak |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| none | solved | 0.128 | 0.053 |\n| 0.06 | **slack** | 0.128 | 0.053 |\n| 0.04 | **binding** | 0.174 | 0.040 |\n| 0.04, budget 2 | **infeasible** | — | — |\n\nA binding cap *raises* total cases: capacity is paid for in infections.\n\n::: {.notes}\nThe middle two rows are the teaching moment. Tightening a constraint the optimum\nalready satisfies changes nothing; tightening it past that point costs you the\nobjective. And with too small a budget the problem is simply infeasible — the\nsolver says so rather than returning something plausible.\n:::\n\n## Two controls, different mechanisms\n\n- $u(t)$ reduces transmission **while applied**; budget $\\int u \\le B$.\n- $v(t)$ removes susceptibles **permanently**; supply $\\int v \\le V$.\n\nAdding vaccination cuts final cases by 76% against the same NPI budget alone.\n\n::: {.notes}\nThe asymmetry is the point: transmission reduction lapses the moment it stops,\nwhereas a vaccinated susceptible stays removed. That changes when the optimiser\nprefers to spend each control, which is what exercise 2 asks about.\n:::\n\n::: {.notes}\nThe limitation is a modeling boundary, not a missing macro. Use a supported\ntranscription for the deterministic nonlinear problem and a different method\nwhen stochastic nonlinear dynamics are essential.\n:::\n\n",
